Name: preferredAppearance

Type: property

Syntax: set the preferredAppearance to {system | light | dark}
Syntax: get the preferredAppearance

Summary:
Forces the application to use a light or dark appearance, or lets it follow
the operating system.

Introduced: 0.9.16

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- in the mainstack of an application that should always be dark
on preOpenStack
   set the preferredAppearance to "dark"
end preOpenStack

Example:
set the preferredAppearance to "system"

Description:
Use the <preferredAppearance> property to decide which appearance the
application draws in. The value is one of:

- "system": follow the operating system's light or dark setting (the default)
- "light": always draw in light mode
- "dark": always draw in dark mode

The property is global to the running process and is never saved in a stack
file. A standalone starts in "system" unless its own script sets the
property, typically in the mainstack's <preOpenStack> handler. The IDE sets
it from its Appearance preference.

Setting the property changes the application's effective appearance, so
everything that depends on it follows: the default colors of objects that
have no explicit colors, native controls such as scrollbars and menu
buttons, the <systemAppearance> property and the <systemAppearanceChanged>
message. Scripts that test `the systemAppearance is "dark"` therefore keep
working when the appearance is forced.

Because the IDE runs stacks in its own process, a stack that sets the
<preferredAppearance> while open in the IDE changes the IDE's appearance too,
in the same way as setting the <lookAndFeel> does.

What the forced appearance covers depends on the platform:

- macOS: the application's NSAppearance is set, so AppKit draws windows,
  menus, panels and native controls in the forced appearance, and every
  appearance query in the engine follows it.
- Windows: the engine draws in the forced appearance (default colors, the
  menu bar, themed controls), the title bars of open and new stack windows
  follow it, and Windows is told to draw its own context menus and tooltips
  for the application in that appearance (Windows 10 1903 or later). With
  "system", the engine follows the Windows "app mode" colour setting.
- Linux: the GTK application-prefer-dark-theme setting is switched, so the
  theme's dark or light variant is used for the engine's native controls and
  the engine's own drawing follows the forced value. When the desktop's theme
  is itself a dark variant (Adwaita-dark, Yaru-dark), "light" uses the base
  theme (Adwaita, Yaru) and "system" puts the desktop's theme back. A GTK
  theme that has no dark or light variant keeps its own colors; only what the
  engine draws and reports changes.

Setting the property always sends <systemAppearanceChanged> to the open
stacks, even when the effective appearance did not change, so handlers can
re-apply their colors. In a headless engine (-ui) the value is stored and
reported but nothing is drawn.

References: systemAppearance (property), systemAppearanceChanged (message),
lookAndFeel (property), preOpenStack (message)
